      "content": "Take a quick look at a standard household door. The metal hinge swings open and closed with zero friction, thousands of times a year. But what happens if you yank that door sideways while kicking the frame? The screws tear straight out of the wood and the metal bends. Your knee is that exact door hinge, while your hip is the heavy wall supporting the entire doorframe.\n\nYour hips and shoulders are ball-and-socket wonders. They can rotate, turn, and absorb massive shock from every angle under the sun. Meanwhile, your knees and elbows are strictly simple hinges built for straight forward-and-backward tracks. When you squat with your knees caving inward toward each other, you are yanking the door sideways on its hinges.\n\nEvery time you jump, squat, or climb stairs, your knee hinge must track smoothly directly over your toes. If your hip socket falls asleep on the job, your knee collapses into the danger zone. Protect your fragile hinges by forcing your hips and shoulders to do the heavy steering work they were built for.",
