      "keyInsight": "Most people think bad technique happens at the bottom of a heavy lift. The funny truth is your fate was sealed before you even bent your knees. Think of any physical movement like driving a car into a narrow mountain tunnel. If your car is aligned straight when you enter, you cruise through without a scratch. If your front tires are crooked at the entrance, you bounce off the walls the entire drive. Your body works on a handful of universal shapes called archetypes. When you lock into a stable archetype at the start, your joints stay protected throughout the entire range of motion. Lose your posture at the entrance, and you spend the rest of the journey crashing into your own joints.",
      "content": "Imagine packing a glass vase into a cardboard box for a cross-country move. If you pack paper tightly around the vase before taping the lid shut, the vase stays safe on bumpy highways. But if you toss the vase loosely into an oversized box, every single turn rattles the fragile glass against the cardboard walls. Your spine and major joints behave just like that delicate vase. Before you bend over to lift groceries or drop down into a squat, you have to pack the box tightly. Setting your joint posture first creates a protective shell that absorbs shock before heavy weight touches your hands.\n\nKelly Starrett calls this journey through movement the tunnel concept. Think of any complex physical task as driving through an underground stone passage. The entrance of the tunnel is your starting setup, while the exit is the completed lift or step. If your steering wheel is centered when you drive in, you fly straight through to the other side. But if you enter with a flat tire and crooked alignment, no amount of frantic steering inside the tunnel can save you from grinding against the concrete walls. Bad setups guarantee messy finishes every single time.\n\nThankfully, you do not have to memorize thousands of unique poses for every sport or chore. Human movement boils down to just a few core body archetypes, like holding your arms overhead or hinging deeply at the hips. When you master a solid overhead shape while putting away a coffee mug, you automatically know how to press a barbell overhead safely. The shape never changes, only the load does. Treat your body like a well-tuned vehicle: set your alignment at the gate, lock your wheels straight, and glide through the tunnel with effortless power.",
