      "headline": "The Skill Ladder: Climbing the Movement Hierarchy",
      "keyInsight": "We love jumping straight into flashy, high-speed workouts before we can even stand up straight from a chair. It is like trying to race a sports car at top speed on a dirt road with bald tires. The human body organizes movement into a strict three-tier ladder of difficulty. Level one movements keep your feet planted and your spine quiet, giving you total control. Level two introduces rotational forces that test your stability. Level three adds explosive speed, wild landings, and unpredictable chaos. The paradox is that people who get hurt doing high-speed stunts rarely have a speed problem. They simply never built the simple slow-speed habits required at the bottom of the ladder.",
