      "keyInsight": "When your car pulls sharply to the left on the highway, you do not just kick the driver-side tire and hope for the best. You pop the hood, inspect the tie rods, check tire pressure, and examine the steering rack. Yet when our hips or shoulders feel tight, we do random, mindless stretches that barely scratch the surface. Real mobility is not about sitting on a yoga mat daydreaming while waiting for a muscle to loosen. It is targeted diagnostic mechanics. Your body has three distinct mechanical systems that get gummed up: the joint capsule, the sliding muscle tissues, and the nerve wiring. If you treat all tightness the same way, you are using a hammer to fix a computer screen.",
      "content": "Think of an old, neglected garden gate swinging on rusted metal hinges. If the gate refuses to open all the way, where is the problem? Maybe thick ivy vines have wrapped tightly around the wooden frame, gluing it shut. Or maybe the metal hinge itself is rusted solid inside its socket. Or perhaps a piece of gravel is jammed underneath the latch. If you pull frantically on the wood without inspecting the hinge or clearing the ivy, you will just splinter the timber. Your stiff joints require that exact same careful diagnosis.\n\nStarrett breaks mobility work into three distinct mechanical systems: Joint Mechanics, Sliding Surfaces, and Motor Control. Joint mechanics deal with the hinge itself. If your hip bone is jammed forward in its socket, stretching the surrounding muscles feels like pulling on a locked door. Using heavy resistance bands creates space inside the joint capsule so the bone can pivot freely again without pinching nearby nerves.\n\nNext come the sliding surfaces, which are like the layers of an onion. Healthy muscles and connective tissue should slide past one another smoothly like oiled silk sheets. When poor posture glues these layers together, deep pressure tools like foam rollers and lacrosse balls break up the sticky fuzz. Finally, your nervous system acts as the electrical switchboard. You must teach your brain how to contract and relax in these newly opened ranges. When you address the hinge, the slide, and the switchboard together, your body unlocks instantly.",
